You want to create a reusable, production-grade `/api-engineering`**&#x20;command/skill** that acts as an API architect and engineering standard for projects.

The goal is not simply to generate CRUD endpoints. It should establish a complete API engineering methodology covering architecture, standards, versioning, security, scalability, performance, documentation, testing, and maintainability.

I would structure it as a standalone, stack-independent engineering skill that can be applied to PHP, Python, Node.js, Next.js, Go, Java, and other backend technologies.

# **Proposed&#x20;**`/api-engineering`**&#x20;skill**

**/api-engineering**

2026 standards

# **API Engineering System**

A reusable engineering framework for designing, building, reviewing, and maintaining production APIs.

1. Research-first API architecture
2. Standards and protocol compliance
3. REST, CRUD, RPC, GraphQL and event-driven APIs
4. Versioning and backward compatibility
5. Security and authentication
6. Scalability and performance engineering
7. Testing, observability and documentation
8. Production readiness and API auditing

## **1. Research-backed standards for 2026**

I checked the official specifications and engineering references. These should form the foundation of the skill.

| Standard / reference                                                                                                                          | What the skill must enforce                          |
| --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| [OpenAPI 3.2.1](https://spec.openapis.org/oas/v3.2?utm_source=chatgpt.com)                                                                    | API contracts and machine-readable documentation     |
| [RFC 9110](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com)                                                                | HTTP semantics, methods, status codes, idempotency   |
| [RFC 9457](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com)                                                                | Standardized API error responses                     |
| [OWASP API Security Top 10](https://devguide.owasp.org/en/07-training-education/07-api-top-ten/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)                       | API-specific security threats                        |
| [OWASP Top 10 2025](https://owasp.org/projects/top-ten?lang=en\&tab=about-the-owasp-top-10\&utm_source=chatgpt.com)                           | General application security                         |
| [Microsoft REST API Design](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/best-practices/api-design?utm_source=chatgpt.com)            | Resource modeling, pagination, filtering, versioning |
| [Microsoft API Implementation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/best-practices/api-implementation?utm_source=chatgpt.com) | Reliability, implementation, testing and monitoring  |
| [RFC 9745](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9745?utm_source=chatgpt.com)                                                              | HTTP API deprecation lifecycle                       |
| [AsyncAPI 3.1](https://github.com/asyncapi/spec/blob/master/README.md?utm_source=chatgpt.com)                                                 | Event-driven and asynchronous API contracts          |

Important discovery

As of November 16, 2026, the official OpenAPI specification lists **3.2.1**, released September 10, 2026. The skill should recognize this as the latest specification while checking compatibility with the actual tooling used by each project.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://spec.openapis.org\&sz=32)

OpenAPI Initiative Publications+1



One important distinction: OWASP API Security Top 10 2023 and OWASP Top 10 2025 are separate references. Your skill should use both rather than confusing their scopes.&#x20;

